.\" Manpage for rpi-sdinfo.
.\" Render with:  man ./docs/rpi-sdinfo.1     or     mandoc docs/rpi-sdinfo.1
.TH RPI-SDINFO 1 "2026-07-08" "rpi-sdinfo 0.9.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rpi-sdinfo \- identify, benchmark and fake-check SD/MMC cards
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rpi-sdinfo
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B rpi-sdbench
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B rpi-sdverify
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rpi-sdinfo
tests the performance and integrity of SD/MMC cards and tries to distinguish genuine
cards from counterfeits by comparing what a card reports about itself against how it
actually behaves. It uses only the Python standard library \(em there are no runtime
dependencies \(em and runs on Raspberry Pi Linux, macOS and Windows.
.PP
It performs up to four kinds of check:
.TP
.B Identity
Capacity, and on a Raspberry Pi the make / model / branding decoded from the CID and CSD
registers, plus filesystem and IO statistics. On macOS and Windows identity is limited to
what the OS exposes, because those platforms do not surface the SD registers.
.TP
.B Benchmark
A native, dependency-free engine (also available standalone as
.BR rpi-sdbench )
measures sequential write throughput and random 4 KiB read/write IOPS, then grades the
result against the card's rated speed class, falling back to A1 when the class is unknown.
.TP
.B Capacity-fraud sweep
An opt-in write-then-verify sweep (also available standalone as
.BR rpi-sdverify ,
the pure-Python cousin of
.BR f3 (1)
and h2testw) fills free space with offset-stamped data and reads it back; a card that lies
about its size fails verification at its true capacity.
.TP
.B Metadata cross-checks
On a Raspberry Pi, the CSD register is decoded and checked for internal contradictions \(em
above all a Standard-Capacity CSD on a card claiming tens of GB, which is impossible per the
SD specification and the signature of a small card reflashed to lie about its size.
As neutral context, it also reports the set of brands the card database has observed shipping
under the card's maker id, to compare against the brand on the card's own label; this is
information only and never a verdict.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-device " DEV"
Storage device to inspect. Linux: block device name (default
.IR mmcblk0 ).
macOS: disk id or mount path (e.g.
.I disk4
or
.IR /Volumes/CARD ).
Windows: a drive (e.g.
.IR E: ).
On macOS, if neither
.B \-\-device
nor
.B \-\-dir
is given, an inserted removable card is auto-detected (preferring an SD-bus reader) and
benchmarked in place of the boot disk.
.TP
.BI \-\-partition " PART"
Linux filesystem partition to inspect (default
.IR <device>p2 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " DIR"
Directory on the card to benchmark. Point this at the mounted card, e.g.
.I /Volumes/CARD
or
.IR E:\e .
.TP
.BI \-\-runs " N"
Number of benchmark runs to average.
.TP
.BI \-\-size\-mb " N"
Test file size in MiB.
.TP
.BI \-\-seconds " N"
Duration of each random IO test.
.TP
.B \-\-block\-sweep
Also measure sequential write throughput at a range of block sizes (4 KiB to 1 MiB) and report the
throughput-vs-block-size curve. A genuine card climbs then plateaus as the block grows past the controller's
page/erase granularity, so a flat, collapsing, or non-scaling curve is a tell for a worn or counterfeit
controller. Non-destructive; off by default. Also available standalone as
.BR "rpi-sdbench \-\-block-sweep" .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-benchmark
Only gather and print card detail; skip the performance test.
.TP
.B \-\-capacity\-check
Also run the capacity-fraud sweep (fills free space, writes and verifies to unmask fakes).
Slow, and adds a little flash wear.
.TP
.BI \-\-capacity\-mb " N"
Cap the capacity sweep to N MiB instead of filling all free space (a quick partial check).
.TP
.B \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt for the capacity sweep (required when non-interactive, e.g. with
.B \-\-json
or
.BR \-\-quiet ).
.TP
.B \-\-full
With
.BR "rpi-sdverify \-\-device" ,
run an exhaustive DESTRUCTIVE full-capacity sweep \(em write then verify
.I every
block of the reported capacity \(em instead of the fast corner probe. Needs no free space (a nearly-full card
is tested by overwriting it) and catches an arbitrary wrap the corner and decimal probes can miss, but takes
hours on a big card (a full write plus a full read). Requires
.BR \-\-yes ,
and refuses a mounted device.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " text|json"
Output format. In
.I json
mode the full result is the only thing written to stdout, so it pipes cleanly to
.BR jq (1);
all progress goes to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Shortcut for
.BR "\-\-format json" .
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress the report; the exit code still reflects PASS/FAIL.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Also dump the verbatim sources (full
.BR dumpe2fs (8)
/ registers / diskutil, decoded CSD, raw benchmark samples) for debugging.
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-db " [PATH]"
Append this run to a local SQLite database (default
.IR ~/.rpi-sdinfo/results.db ),
building a queryable history of every card tested. Local-only.
.TP
.BI \-\-db\-query " [PATH]"
Summarise the saved run history and exit instead of testing a card. Honours
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR \-\-color ", " \-\-no\-color
Force or disable colour output. Colour also honours the
.B NO_COLOR
and
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
environment conventions and switches off when output is piped.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a full option summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Pass \(em all checks that ran succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Fail \(em a benchmark grade, capacity sweep or metadata cross-check failed (a likely fake or
under-performing card).
.TP
.B 2
Usage or IO error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Full detail on a Raspberry Pi (root reads the registers):
.B sudo rpi-sdinfo
.TP
Benchmark a card mounted on macOS, machine-readable:
.B rpi-sdinfo \-\-json \-\-dir /Volumes/CARD | jq .grade.pass
.TP
Unmask a fake by filling and verifying the whole card:
.B rpi-sdinfo \-\-dir /Volumes/CARD \-\-capacity\-check
.TP
Fast, destructive raw-device fake sniff (unmount first):
.B rpi-sdverify \-\-device /dev/disk4 \-\-yes
.TP
Exhaustive, destructive raw-device sweep of every block (slow; catches arbitrary wraps):
.B rpi-sdverify \-\-device /dev/disk4 \-\-full \-\-yes
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.rpi-sdinfo/results.db
Default SQLite history database written by
.BR \-\-save\-db .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, disables colour output.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
If set, forces colour output even when not writing to a terminal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR f3write (1),
.BR f3read (1),
.BR fio (1),
.BR jq (1),
.BR dumpe2fs (8)
